## Files you write
- app.json: id (slug of the plan name), name, description (one sentence), space ("{{.Space}}"), version 1, kit 2, agent {name, file: "agent.md", model: "{{.AgentModel}}"}, records, capabilities, triggers, commands, budget. Record ids = the plan's data ids. Budget from: {{.Budget}} (month, currency "USD", per_run, at_cap "ask", models). Connectors{{if .Connectors}} you may use, only when the plan needs them: {{join .Connectors ", "}}{{else}}: none are connected, so declare none; nothing is sent{{end}}.
- screens/<id>.json (root keys screen, title, nav, body) and runs/<id>.yaml: one per plan line; `screen` and `run` equal the file name.
- agent.md: front matter `name`, `model`, `tools`, then plain instructions.
Every name used must exist: screens in `open`, runs in triggers, commands and actions, record types in `to`, binds and capabilities, fields in columns, fields, set and queries.

## Records
- Pick the real type for each field: money, date, datetime, number, bool, enum (values in the order a person works through them), email, phone, url, longtext for notes, `ref`/`refs` with `to` for a link to another record type (never a text field holding a name), `person` only for a member of this app who owns or is assigned (default "$me" for an owner). A date with a time of day is one `datetime`, never a date plus a text.
- Each record type has `label`, `plural`, `title` (a required text field that names the record; where nothing is natural, as for a booking or a stock movement, add a short `summary` text field and make that the title). Do not make refs `required`. Add `index: true` to status, date and ref fields you sort or filter by; give a status enum a `default`.
- A yes/no a person would query, such as "low on stock", is a `bool` with `computed` (CEL over the other fields: `"stock <= reorder_level"`), listed by query token `low:true`. `computed` and `when` read only fields of the record, never the clock; for age use a token like `last_touch<-7d`. Name fields bare: `changed('stage') && stage == 'Won'`, never `record.stage`.
- An `ai` field only when the plan calls for it; its label ends "· AI".

## Screens
- One screen per plan screen. Every screen has the same `nav`: one SectionTabs whose `items` are the screen titles in plan order (with a `label`).
- A record screen is master and detail: a Row with a Stack (title Text and a primary "New <thing>" Button that does `records.create` with `ask` for its title and one or two key fields, then the RecordList) and a Panel, `when` "$selection.<list>.count == 1", holding a RecordPage, a Form that edits it (every editable field in `fields`, an `on.submit` `records.update` whose `set` maps each field to `$input.<field>`) and a "Delete <thing>…" Button with `records.delete` and `confirm`.
- RecordList: `id`, `label`, `bind` records with a `query` (sort:-field or sort:field last), `columns` (at most 6 real fields, title first), `query: true`, and an `empty` slot Empty whose text tells the owner what to add. A status enum with three or more values gets `views` ["Board by <field>","List"], `meter` and `groupBy` on that field. A date-driven record sorts by its date. Query tokens are plain words only: `stage:Proposal,Won`, `status:-Done`, `owner:me`, `low:true`, `sort:-amount` last. No variables, no functions, no dates like $now.
- A subset the plan says to show (running low, upcoming, overdue) is a second RecordList with a filtering query under a Text heading, above the main list. A summary the plan asks for uses Chart (`chart: "metric"`, `agg`) or a second RecordList with a filtering query; BulkBar `list` names its RecordList and holds Buttons that act on `$selection.<list>.ids`. Actions in `on`: open, records.create, records.update, records.delete, runs.start, approve, tools.call, ai.sort, agent.ask. `ask` maps `input` names to a field type; ask only for text, number, money, date, datetime, enum, email, phone or url fields, never a ref or person, which are set in the panel's Form. Values may use $me $selection.<id>.id|ids|count $input.x $now.
- Every interactive part has a `label`. Destructive labels end in "…" and carry `confirm`.

## Runs, commands and the agent
- A run has `run`, `title`, `budget.per_run`, `params`, `steps`. A step has an `id` and exactly one action: `agent` with `prompt` and an `output` schema, `tool`, `records.query|create|update|delete`, `wait`, or nested `steps` with `loop: {over, as, max}`. Every outbound tool step carries `gate: {approval: send, who: $me, title: "Approval needed: send ..."}`.
- A summary or answer the owner reads is no run: put a Button on that screen with `agent.ask` (label ends "· AI"). Send nothing nobody asked to send.
- A time-based wish ("every Monday", "the day before") is a `schedule` trigger with `cron` plus a command that starts the same run by hand; a record event is `record.changed` with `type` and `when`. A command either starts a `run` or does a list of `do` actions, and its `for` is a record type.
- capabilities: `records` lists every type as "rw" (or "r"), `connectors` only those the runs and agent use, with `approve` ["send"] for sending, `ai` only the verbs used. agent.md `tools` is a subset of what capabilities allow (`records.query`, `records.get`, `records.update`, `<connector>.draft`, `<connector>.send`).
- agent.md: what the app is for, the agent's jobs with its records, that it names what it read and changed, that every send waits for "Approval needed: send", and rules 2 and 9. Under 12 lines.

